<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Defs>
  <!--
    The trait the Medic armor grants on equip (its ApparelExtension.traitsOnEquip), in the
    Mechlord/Alpha Mechs idiom of an own trait beside VFEP_WarcasketTrait: VFEP's trait blocks
    Caring, which is Doctor, and a child apparel extension can only add to what a granted trait
    blocks, never subtract. Docs/Specs/Medic-Work-Trait.md is the design record.

    Mirrored at startup, do not tune here. WorkProfile.Mirror (Source/1.6/WorkProfile.cs, run
    first in Startup.Run on every play-data load) overwrites this def from VFEP_WarcasketTrait
    as the full mod list leaves it, so "Warcaskets can work!", "can haul!" and Hardworking
    Warcaskets shape the Medic exactly as they shape a Siegebreaker pilot without knowing we
    exist. The contract:
      - disabledWorkTags = VFEP's, with ManualDumb swapped for Cleaning (VFEP's hauling
        exemption is a Harmony postfix keyed on its own def, so a copied ManualDumb would lose
        hauling; ManualDumb covers only Hauling and Cleaning) and Caring removed.
      - disabledWorkTypes = VFEP's, plus every Caring work type other than Doctor that the tags
        no longer block (Childcare under Biotech, any modded one), only when VFEP's tags still
        carried Caring; a patch mod that opened Caring for every warcasket opens it here too.
      - degreeDatas[0]: every public field but the text, degree and commonality copied from
        VFEP's degree, and the TraitExtension's fields copied from VFEP's extension. Both copies are full
        overwrites, nulls included, so the Medic carries exactly what VFEP's trait carries on
        the running game version: on 1.7, painFactor 0 and RestFallRateFactor 1.3 on the degree;
        on 1.6, painFactor 0 on the extension and no stat factors.
    The values below are the mirror's result from an unpatched VFEP trait on 1.7, so the def
    reads correctly on its own. Every field used exists on 1.6 as well.

    The extension omits painFactor on purpose, as VFEP's 1.7 copy does: VEF's TraitExtension
    leaves the float uninitialised and its CalculatePain postfix multiplies pain by any value
    other than 1, so an omitted painFactor is zero pain. The mirror overwrites it from VFEP's
    extension before any pawn exists; the XML value would only ever apply if VFEP's trait had no
    extension at all. apparelExclusiveTrait keeps trait-adding mods and
    the trait-rolling code paths from handing this trait to a pawn outside the suit (VEF's
    TraitSet.GainTrait prefix admits it only while a worn apparel lists it in traitsOnEquip).

    The description must hold under every profile a patch mod can leave, so it states the suit's
    intent rather than listing work types, and does not promise childcare.
  -->
  <TraitDef>
    <defName>MDWC_WarcasketTrait_Medic</defName>
    <commonality>0</commonality>
    <disabledWorkTags>
      <li>ManualSkilled</li>
      <li>Cleaning</li>
      <li>Animals</li>
      <li>Artistic</li>
      <li>Intellectual</li>
    </disabledWorkTags>
    <disabledWorkTypes>
      <li MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Biotech">Childcare</li>
    </disabledWorkTypes>
    <degreeDatas>
      <li>
        <label>medic warcasket</label>
        <description>{PAWN_nameDef} is entombed in a medic warcasket. No amount of pain will have an effect on {PAWN_objective}, and {PAWN_pronoun} can never go into a pain shock or drop unconscious due to pain. The suit is built around treating the wounded, so {PAWN_nameDef} keeps the use of {PAWN_possessive} medical and social faculties; the bulk of the armor and the energy it takes to move it make it a poor fit for most other work.</description>
        <painFactor>0</painFactor>
        <statFactors>
          <RestFallRateFactor>1.3</RestFallRateFactor>
        </statFactors>
      </li>
    </degreeDatas>
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="VEF.Pawns.TraitExtension">
        <apparelExclusiveTrait>true</apparelExclusiveTrait>
      </li>
    </modExtensions>
  </TraitDef>
</Defs>
